WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.620
Secure ASP.NET Core Web API development is a lifecycle concern.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:14.040
It starts when a team decides what the API should expose, who should call it, which data it can return, and which operations deserve extra review.

3
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:21.500
It continues through implementation, deployment, monitoring, dependency maintenance, and incident response.

4
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:29.920
A safe API is not only code that compiles; it is a service whose behavior is understandable and controlled across its whole life.

5
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.760
APIs sit at many trust boundaries.

6
00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:47.740
A browser, mobile app, automation script, partner service, background worker, identity provider, database, queue, file store, administrator console, and third-party integration may all interact with the API under different assumptions.

7
00:00:47.840 --> 00:01:00.320
When data, identity, files, commands, or configuration cross one of those boundaries, the API should treat them as untrusted or at least unverified until the server checks them for the current purpose.

8
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:07.000
There is a meaningful difference between a feature existing and a feature being safely exposed.

9
00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:12.780
Internal code may assume a trusted caller, a narrow workflow, or controlled inputs.

10
00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:23.280
A public or partner-facing endpoint has to survive broader caller behavior, version drift, automation, retries, partial failures, and operational mistakes.

11
00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:35.380
Exposing a capability through an API should trigger review of authorization, validation, resource cost, error behavior, logging, and documentation.

12
00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:50.460
ASP.NET Core provides strong security building blocks for authentication, authorization, routing, model binding, validation, CORS, rate limiting, logging, configuration, and hosting.

13
00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:56.080
Those features help when they are configured intentionally and reviewed as part of the application design.

14
00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:04.340
They do not automatically decide which clients should exist, which endpoints should be public, or which fields should be accepted from a caller.

15
00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:11.260
A practical API security program connects prevention, detection, maintainability, and responsible engineering.

16
00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:18.240
Prevent misuse with narrow endpoints, clear models, server-side validation, and consistent authorization.

17
00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:22.900
Detect problems through safe logs, correlation identifiers, and monitoring.

18
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:29.880
Keep the API maintainable through clear versioning, reviewed dependencies, and documented operational assumptions.

19
00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:37.340
That combination is what lets teams improve API security without turning every release into a special event.
